  "is_sufficient": false,
  "knowledge_gaps": [
    "Padding-ID ablation may introduce artifacts: convolution biases and normalization can produce nonzero hidden states at masked positions. Bands that include the final item also conflict with final-nonpadding readout. Define whether lag zero is excluded, retain the original readout anchor, and handle histories emptied by masking.",
    "Receptive-field accounting must include residual branches and any normalization or pooling that mixes temporal positions. The report lacks exact lag bands, eligible-history rules, aggregation weights, uncertainty estimates, runtime limits, and a reproducible sampling procedure.",
    "One positive and one negative support paired margins and ranking flips, not meaningful HR@10 or top-10 overlap. Those metrics require an explicitly available larger candidate set. Training-loader sensitivity also cannot establish generalization or harmful reliance on older events.",
    "The originality rating is reasonable, but future potential remains speculative and difficulty may be understated until padding behavior and predictor consistency are verified. Zero-ID masking should be compared with controlled replacements before its effects motivate recency regularization."
  ],
  "follow_up_queries": [
    {
      "reason": "Determine whether the intervention measures temporal reliance or padding artifacts, and specify a consistent readout policy.",
      "query": "In NextItNet sequential recommendation with left-padded histories, how do zero-item embeddings, convolution biases, residual connections, and normalization affect inference-time internal padding ablations? What controls distinguish masking artifacts from history sensitivity, particularly when preserving the original final-position readout and excluding lag zero?"
    },
    {
      "reason": "Check whether the proposed receptive-field verification is valid for the actual architecture.",
      "query": "How should the effective dependency range of a residual dilated causal convolution recommender such as NextItNet be calculated and verified when normalization may aggregate across temporal positions? Which architectural conditions guarantee that editing inputs outside the convolutional receptive field leaves final-position scores unchanged?"
    },
    {
      "reason": "Develop a reproducible evaluation that avoids conflating sensitivity with recommendation improvement.",
      "query": "What experimental controls and statistical reporting are recommended for frozen sequential-recommender history ablations: predefined lag bands, matched numbers of removed interactions, history-length strata, paired user bootstrap, and replacement controls? How should training positive-negative margin changes be distinguished from fixed-candidate validation HR@10 changes and evidence that older history is harmful?"
    }
  ]
}
